A||||23||CUTTER||Please Help!!!!!||||1||
Z||000000||CUTTER||12-14-1999||11:18 PM||||  I just started noticing that when I slam the gas on my 87gt auto. that it takes forever to get up to speed. Its like I need to get out and push it when I go up hills. Like its only running on 4 cyls. or something. Could it be my spark plugs and wires? I got my car about 6 months ago and put well over 15,000 miles on it and never changed the plugs or wires. and I have whatsoever no top end after 70mph. it takes about 20 seconds to get to 80-90 full throttle. Any help would be very much appreciated!!!!    Shane||||reg||1
Z||000001||CUTTER||12-14-1999||11:22 PM||||I was also woundering if it could be the trans? because I used to neutral drop it alot when I 1st got it but know the damage it can do and havent done it since.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||12-15-1999||01:08 AM||||It could be the transmission slipping. Is your fluid burned/dark looking or have a burned odor? <P>Or, I have found a general lack of power with no misfire is often related to fuel, air, and exhaust. Clogged fuel filter, clogged air filter, bad fuel pump, clogged catalytic converter.  When my converter was bad the headers (6 cylinder) got red hot from back pressure.  When my fuel filter was bad (a different car)I had no power up hills (same also for cat.).   ||||reg||1
Z||000003||CUTTER||12-15-1999||03:24 PM||||Now my car has less power then it did, I bet a 3cyl geo metro could beat my car now how its running. Im afraid its going to let me sit somewhere. will the trans do this if its going bad or bad? my car also sounds differnt it sounds more quiet then it did and alot more deeper in tone. When I slam on the gas it has a sluggish acceleration. Wont hardly even go. What do you think it is??? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||12-15-1999||03:33 PM||||Change in exhaust tone! I,d guess Catalytic Converter clogged as Ed mentioned.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||12-15-1999||03:40 PM||||<BR>There should be some easy way to determine whether this is an engine problem or a tranny problem.<P>When the car is OUT of gear and you rev it up, does the throttle response seem any different than it used to? If it's still sluggish, then at least you've narrowed your search for the problem down to your engine.<P>If you're absolutely positive that the engine does seem as responsive (no missing, etc) when out of gear, then it's the tranny.<P>Of course, that doesn't really solve anything yet.||||reg||1
Z||000006||dogtired||12-15-1999||03:50 PM||||I pulled the 2 bolts where the exhaust header connects to the rest of the system. And then drove the car (my 87SE), that is how I determined it was my CAT. It is really loud and you don't want to leave it unhooked long, it causes a lot of heat where it shouldn't be, but is relative easy. As much problems as I have had with CATs I would start there. GOOD luck.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by dogtired (edited 12-15-1999).]||||reg||6
Z||000007||DRH||12-15-1999||04:49 PM||||Cutter, From your description I don't think it's the tranny. A bad tranny would usually mean it is slipping. This would mean the engine is reving quicker than normal but you're not taking off as fast. Your description sounds more like the engine doesn't want to rev at all. That in combination with the changed exhaust sound would make a clogged cat my first suspect as a couple of people already pointed out. <P>Whatever it is, it sounds like it has a pretty high leave you sit potential. I'd check it out, pronto.<P>dogtired's testing method should work. It may not run exactly right with no exhaust but you should see a major power increase if it is the cat. As he mentioned, it will be REALLY loud! In other words... don't do it late at night or early in the morning if you want to stay friendly with your neighbors  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck, David.||||reg||1
Z||000008||dogtired||12-15-1999||05:26 PM||||Cutter<BR>Just a side note here:<BR>Do you know what is 21 inches long?<BR>That is how much pipe you need to replace the CAT with, and it sounds GREAT.<BR>No Smog testing here in Kansas? Lets see $3 bucks for a piece of pipe or $100 for a new CAT?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula||12-15-1999||06:46 PM||||or $30 to gut the cat and put a new piece behind it. My car sounds like a 305 (5.0) v8 now.||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||12-15-1999||07:16 PM||||Does anyone have a Hi-flow cat on their car?  If so, please tell me the specs and if you are happy with it.  <P>Many Thanks,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000011||CUTTER||12-15-1999||09:31 PM||||I think it is the CAT but how do I take those "2bolts" off and where are they that DogTired said? what do you think I should get? a regular stock CAT or one of those Hi-flow CATS. or what? what is the difference in performance? and dont forget price.<BR>You guys are great. Thanks alot, Shane||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||12-15-1999||10:47 PM||||Unless you're eventually planning on putting a better flowing exhaust system (including ported manifolds or headers), a high flow cat is probably not needed. An efficient exhaust system does help a little with fuel economy and increases power.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-15-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||CUTTER||12-15-1999||10:51 PM||||The only thing I have on is a flowmaster muffler.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||01:38 AM||||Unless you live in one of those dumb-a** states that require an inspection, I would go with the $3 pipe.  I have a friend who works for a major muffler mfr who made me some fake mufflers for my last truck.  They are hollowed out, piece of pipe down the middle with the factory welds.  No one (DOT) could tell the difference.  Damn, it sounded good!||||reg||1
Z||000015||AL||12-16-1999||02:15 AM||||change the fuel filter, when they start clogging they cut performance in half||||reg||1
Z||000016||dogtired||12-16-1999||09:32 AM||||Cutter<BR>In your first post you said 87GT therefore I assume it is a V6?     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>That being SO, Open your deck lid and stand by your air cleaner. Look over it and down, you will see your exhaust pipe, the O2 sensor and just below that 2bolts with big springs around them. 15mm heads get at them from the underside. Spray liberal amounts of penetrating oil on the square nuts before you start (maybe 1 hour) I twisted one off on my 87SE so I got a 12 point socket on the square nut and purposly broke it off, that way I could put a new nut and bolt there.<BR>Another Idea! Look at your CAT, is there clanps on both ends, or is it welded? If clamped you are in luck. Well Kind-of, I took the 2 bolts out of the one end and removed the clamp on the other. With CAT in hand and a long screwdriver or prybar you can break all the stuff out of the cat. (just crunch and break till there is nothing in there) If it is welded there is a exhaust pipe cutter that has 4 cutting wheels on it and you only have to turn it 1/4 turn, back and forth sort of like a pipe cutter, (mine was about $20, ten years ago) Then put it back on   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Looks like you have a CAT but sounds and preforms lots better. I have a straight pipe 21 inches on one car and an empty cat on the other.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> I would rather take a beating than work on an Exhaust!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">  GOOD LUCK<p>[This message has been edited by dogtired (edited 12-16-1999).]||||reg||6
Z||000017||Mark||12-18-1999||08:22 AM||||Before disassembling your exhaust system, I would check the ignition timing.  You may have jumped a tooth on the timing chain/gears.  A change in valve timing (as well as ignition timing) will have a profound effect on engine power.  ||||reg||1
Z||000018||CUTTER||12-18-1999||11:29 AM||||Well thanx to you guys I found out it was the CAT. I was gonna do it my self but thought....ahhh what the hell I have extra money and didnt feel like doing it in the freezing cold because it broke down on me about 5 miles from my house. the garage towed it and put a new CAT on and charged me 165 bucks. Is that a good price for 7-9 miles of towing plus to put a CAT on?<BR>Thanx again, Shane||||reg||1
Z||000019||fiero56||12-18-1999||11:56 AM||||Sounds fair to me, but depends on what quality cat they put on.||||reg||1
Z||000020||dogtired||12-20-1999||05:37 PM||||Sounds like you did good.<BR>I would have figured about $35 + for the tow and in the neighborhood of $100 for the CAT depending on quality and then the rest in labor. NOT BAD!  <BR>But Hay if you have extra bucks, and they aren't any big deal, let me know & I will send my address   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Good going, its back on the road.||||reg||6
